    "result_text": "Done. The change is complete and the suite is green.\n\n## What I did\n\nThe acceptance test does `GET /api/owners/{id}` and asserts `$.bulkSignupWarning` is `false` for a fresh create. The codebase already establishes a clear pattern: read-only derived owner fields are declared in `openapi.yml` (generating the `OwnerDto`) and populated by small, single-purpose `@AfterReturning` aspects (`OwnerMembershipNumberAdvice`, `OwnerNamesakeCountAdvice`).\n\nI followed that pattern rather than touching any existing method:\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a `bulkSignupWarning` boolean property (`default: false`, `readOnly`) to the `Owner` schema, so the generated DTO gets the field, getter, and setter.\n\n2. **New `OwnerBulkSignupWarningAdvice.java`** \u2014 a cohesive one-method aspect that, on the way out of `addOwner`/`getOwner`, counts owners whose `registrationDate` is today (reusing `BusinessDay.onOrNextBusinessDay`, the same accumulation path as `OwnerDailyLimitAdvice`) and sets the flag to `count > 80`.\n\n## Structural impact\n\nKept minimal: no existing function was modified (so no `WMC_other` from any large class is paid), and the new advice is a standalone class with essentially zero surrounding complexity. The only edits are one schema block and one new small file.\n\nTest result: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0 \u2014 BUILD SUCCESS` (AcceptanceTest is the only concrete test class; `AcceptanceBase` is abstract).",
